// CARD a4-01 · SEE: real stock monitors; the price line draws and plunges; on "nineteen" a red tag −19% snaps to the low: FACEBOOK · 26 JULY 2018 · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@ticker_crash;bg:stock_screen_chart_up_15;draw:1.4;pts:.8,.82,.86,.84,.88,.9,.86,.4,.32;low:−19%;go:first;lab:FACEBOOK (FB) · 26 JULY 2018;sub:about $119B of market value in one day;in:right"
{tense} So what does a scandal like that actually cost a company like this?
{grave} The first bill came from the stock **market**.
On the twenty-sixth of July, twenty eighteen, Facebook shares fell nineteen percent in a single **day**.

// CARD a4-02 · SEE: real trading screens, dim; one long bar, FACEBOOK MARKET VALUE $630B; on "About" its end peels off and drops: −$119B IN ONE DAY · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@bar_slice;bg:stock_finance_exchange_floor_04;total:630;part:119;go:billion;tlab:FACEBOOK MARKET VALUE · 25 JUL 2018 · $630B;plab:−$119B IN ONE DAY;unit:B;out:right;push:.008"
About one hundred and nineteen billion dollars of market value disappeared, the largest one-day loss for any US company at the **time**.

// CARD a4-03 · SEE: a closed folder on the desk; on "fined" its flap lifts and the real UK Monetary Penalty Notice of 24 Oct 2018 slides out; a stamp lands on it: £500,000 · THE LEGAL MAXIMUM · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@folder_reveal;doc:meta_doc_ico_mpn_2018_p1;crop:0,0,1,.6;stamp:UK REGULATOR;go:fined;after:£500,000 · THE LEGAL MAXIMUM;go2:pounds;in:right;push:.018;pan:.005"
In October, Britain's data regulator fined it five hundred thousand **pounds**.
It found that at least one million British users had been put at **risk**.

// CARD a4-04 · SEE: a real office, dim; chalk tally marks scratch themselves, one a minute, to seventeen: ≈ 17 MINUTES OF PROFIT; £500,000 per The Register · SEAM out right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@years_tally;pic:meta_art_office_night;n:17;go:maximum;span:1.4;lab:≈ 17 MINUTES OF PROFIT;lab2:£500,000 · per The Register, 2018;at2:tech;out:right;push:.008"
That was the legal maximum. One tech news site worked out it was about seventeen minutes of Facebook's **profit**. (J)

// CARD a4-05 · SEE: the real Palace of Westminster at dusk; a paper card rises and the verbatim line of the committee report types on it, "digital gangsters" underlined · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@quote_card_type;bg:meta_palace_westminster_dusk;q:“Companies like Facebook should not be allowed to behave like ‘digital gangsters’ in the online world.”;span:3.5;h:.44;hl:gangsters;go:committee;rise:0;by:DCMS COMMITTEE FINAL REPORT · 18 FEB 2019~refused three invitations to give evidence;in:right;push:.018;pan:.005"
In February twenty nineteen, a committee of the British Parliament called companies like Facebook "digital **gangsters**."
Zuckerberg had refused its invitations to give evidence three **times**. (J)

// CARD a4-06 · SEE: the real FTC order of 24 July 2019, page 3, on a dark desk; the camera travels to the judgment line and a highlighter runs across FIVE BILLION DOLLARS ($5,000,000,000) · SEAM out right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@doc_read;doc:meta_doc_ftc_order_2019_p3;crop:0,.05,1,.45;marks:.17,.155,.76,.18@Trade;zoom:Trade;z:.6;out:right;push:.018;pan:-.005"
Then, on the twenty-fourth of July, twenty nineteen, the Federal Trade Commission announced a five billion dollar **penalty**.

// CARD a4-07 · SEE: NEW: two long bars, ONE YEAR OF REVENUE · 2018 and ONE YEAR OF PROFIT · 2018; on "nine" the penalty lights red from the end of each: 9 % and 23 % · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@revenue_strip;bg:stock_screen_spreadsheet_10;bars:ONE YEAR OF REVENUE · 2018^9^But~ONE YEAR OF PROFIT · 2018^23^But;lab:THE $5B PENALTY, MEASURED;note:largest privacy penalty ever imposed at the time · FTC;in:right"
It was the largest privacy penalty ever imposed at the time. But it was about nine percent of Facebook's revenue for one **year**.

// CARD a4-08 · SEE: the pages of the real 2019 FTC order flick past and stop on page 24, CERTIFICATIONS; the camera reads in and the highlighter runs: the Independent Privacy Committee on "committee", each full fiscal quarter on "personally", signed by the Principal Executive Officer on "sign" · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@page_turn;pages:meta_doc_ftc_order_2019_p1,meta_doc_ftc_order_2019_p3,meta_doc_ftc_order_2019_p24;stop:order;marks:.34,.847,.594,.861@committee~.518,.83,.694,.844@personally~.389,.882,.715,.896@sign;zoom:committee;z:.8;lab:EVERY QUARTER;out:right;push:.008"
The order also put a new privacy committee on Facebook's board, and made Zuckerberg personally sign off on privacy every **quarter**.

// CARD a4-09 · SEE: the real SEC complaint of 24 July 2019 on a dark desk; on "may" a red line strikes the words "may be"; KNEW FOR ABOUT TWO YEARS · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@redact_bars;doc:meta_doc_sec_complaint_2019_p9;crop:0,.74,1,.94;strike:.7,.83,.95,.855;sat:may;go:known;rows:0;in:right;push:.018;pan:.005"
The same day, the SEC added one hundred million dollars, because Facebook had told investors that user data "may be" misused, when it already **knew**.
It had known for about two **years**. (J)

// CARD a4-10 · SEE: a real hand scrolling a phone, dim; a red price tag on a string reads $725M; on "Shared" it spins round: A FEW DOLLARS EACH · SEAM out right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@gift_tag_flip;clip:stock_ui_phone_night_01;front:$725M;back:A FEW DOLLARS EACH;go:Shared;out:right;push:.018;pan:-.005"
In twenty twenty-two, Meta agreed to pay seven hundred and twenty-five million dollars to settle a class action brought by **users**.
Shared among up to eighty-seven million people, that was a few dollars **each**.

// CARD a4-11 · SEE: index cards drop onto a dark desk one per sentence: TEXAS / facial recognition / $1.4B · 2024, IRELAND / data transfers / €1.2B · 2023, SHAREHOLDERS / sought $8B / SETTLED · 2025 · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@case_cards;rows:TEXAS/biometrics · 2024/$1.4 BILLION~IRELAND/data to the US · 2023/€1.2 BILLION~SHAREHOLDERS/sought $8B/SETTLED;gos:Texas,Ireland,shareholders;title:OTHER PRIVACY BILLS;in:right"
In other privacy cases, Texas got one point four billion **dollars**.
And Ireland's regulator fined it one point two billion **euros**.
In twenty twenty-five, shareholders who had sued Zuckerberg and other executives for eight billion dollars settled in the middle of the trial, on terms nobody **disclosed**.

